.TH ONEKEYIRC 1 "2026-11-19" "onekeyirc 1.0-a" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
onekeyirc \- command-oriented terminal IRC client
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B onekeyirc
.RI [ OPTION ... ]
.br
.B onekeyirc \-\-resume
.RI [ NUMBER | SOCKET ]
.br
.B onekeyirc \-\-sessions
.br
.B onekeyirc \-\-detach\-session
.I NUMBER
.br
.B onekeyirc \-\-close\-session
.I NUMBER
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
onekeyirc is a command\-oriented terminal IRC client without a curses dependency. / from its live log opens Command; ordinary typing opens Compose with a live retained tail. Multiple server profiles, channels and separate private queries share one process.
.PP
After the welcome, /connect irc.example.org and /join #channel starts a direct conversation. /saveconf remembers healthy profiles and the selected default for a later bare /connect, without enabling autoconnect. /quickstart and /panel provide guided setup. /help ls lists topics; /help COMMAND explains a command, while /help /ls explains the local list command.
.PP
Terminal interrupt and suspend key assignments are inherited without modification, but signal generation is off by default. Ctrl\-C and Ctrl\-Z cancel unfinished input; the panel 0 Use signals setting can enable signals when wanted. Literal Ctrl\-D is ignored unless explicitly bound in main chat/command input. Custom hotkeys are empty by default; see HOTKEYS below. See KEYS and SESSIONS below for suspension, detach and recovery behavior.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-idle
Use the minimalist interface, the only frontend in onekeyirc. \-N/\-\-ncurses is rejected, including in management commands.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-server HOST
Select the primary server hostname or unbracketed numeric IPv4/IPv6 address, not an IRC URL. Without a configured server, start disconnected.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-port PORT
TCP port 1..65535. The usual defaults are verified TLS 6697 and explicitly plaintext IRC 6667. A port number alone does not disable TLS.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-channel LIST
Direct\-profile autojoin after registration/authentication; comma\-separated lists and repeated options work. Quote #channel for the shell. Bouncers restore memberships instead. With no channel, the server view shows registration and MOTD.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-nick NICK
Nickname override. Otherwise use a valid system login name or user without a nickname prompt.
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-config FILE
Select primary configuration and its adjacent saved\-server catalog. An explicitly selected file must exist except with \-\-init\-config. Config values are literal; the application does not expand variables, tilde or shell syntax. The invoking shell still performs normal argument quoting/expansion.
.TP
.B \-\-tls
Require certificate\-chain and hostname/address verification. This is the default; there is no verification bypass or silent downgrade.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Explicit unencrypted IRC. Configured credential authentication is refused over this transport.
.TP
.B \-\-ca\-file FILE
Use PEM authorities instead of the TLS library's default trust store. The custom file must be nonempty, regular and at most 16 MiB; ordinary symlinks to regular CA files are allowed. Nonregular inputs and detected growth/shrink fail, and PEM parsing cannot prompt for a password. Regular/network\-filesystem latency is not bounded. See TLS below.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-connect
Suppress every profile's startup connection, or leave offline simulators stopped. /connect HOST connects later; bare /connect uses the selected profile.
.TP
.B \-\-background
Start a resumable worker without a terminal or welcome keypress. Configure saved autoconnect profiles or supply \-\-server; this option does not enable autoconnect, and \-\-no\-connect wins. Success confirms local setup, not remote IRC login. Do not combine with init/check\-config or session\-management operations.
.TP
.B \-\-reconnect
Retry temporary DNS/connection failures for this process without initiating connection or saving a preference. Delays are 5,10,20,40,80,160, then 300 seconds; 60 registered seconds resets backoff. /disconnect cancels retry and /connect starts explicitly. Certificate/authentication/configuration/protocol failures remain held. Pending chat is discarded. New connections repeat authentication and enabled startup commands; direct profiles replay autojoin, bouncers restore membership. Staged changes need an explicit disconnected /connect.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-mode
Interactive simulation using normal settings without IRC/DCC networking, DNS, credential/CA reads, GPG or config/count writes. Explicit Background can still create local session IPC. Missing connection fields do not prompt. \-\-no\-connect leaves simulators stopped; otherwise they start even with autoconnect off. Exit and restart without this option for real networking. DCC Send/Accept is refused before file/socket access.
.TP
.B \-\-resume [NUMBER|SOCKET]
Attach the sole verified detached job, or choose a listed number. \-\-resume=NUMBER also works. Multiple or unverified candidates prevent automatic selection; attached jobs remain busy until detached. A compatible absolute socket path is accepted. Input/output/error must refer to the same interactive terminal. Resume preserves retained state; it does not reconstruct a dead process or update a running worker's loaded code.
.TP
.B \-\-sessions
List this account's attached/detached local jobs and availability without a terminal or configuration load. At most 64 slots are probed. Job IDs remain stable during a worker's life and may be reused after exit; they are not PIDs or in\-app server numbers.
.TP
.B \-\-detach\-session NUMBER
Release only the verified job's attached terminal/controller while keeping IRC, transfers, drafts and memory alive. Wait for confirmation, then \-\-resume NUMBER. Also accepts \-\-detach\-session=NUMBER. Use this recovery sequence after a lost SSH terminal rather than ending the whole job.
.TP
.B \-\-close\-session NUMBER
End the whole selected worker: all IRC connections/transfers, unsaved drafts and memory history. Saved configs and existing logs remain. Also accepts \-\-close\-session=NUMBER. Success requires verified cleanup and release of the original lease, not just request acceptance. A timeout may precede later completion; recheck \-\-sessions. A failed request does not remove a live endpoint or signal a guessed PID. Legacy/unresponsive workers can fail.
.TP
.B \-\-init\-config
Create a private editable configuration and exit. Atomic publication refuses any existing object, including one created concurrently. A reported cleanup/sync failure after publication can leave the completed file in place. Connection options can provide initial values.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Validate primary config, overrides, saved catalog and referenced profiles, then exit. Does not open credential sources, run GPG or test DNS, CA loading, TLS, authentication or a live bouncer.
.TP
.B \-\-manual
Print the complete built\-in topic/command reference without a terminal. The shipped MANUAL.txt and doc/onekeyirc\-manual.txt are matching exports.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print concise command\-line help and exit without a terminal.
.TP
.B \-v, \-V, \-\-version
Print version, attribution and license name, then exit without a terminal.
.PP
At normal startup \-\-nick/\-\-port/\-\-channel/\-\-tls/\-\-plain/\-\-ca\-file affect the remembered default. Explicit \-\-server selects the primary. \-\-init\-config and \-\-check\-config apply overrides to the primary. An unavailable remembered default requires \-\-server or repair before applying non\-host overrides.
.PP
A case\-insensitively different \-\-server clears inherited credentials/authentication, custom CA, bouncer identity/automatic\-away choice, autojoin channels and startup commands. Same\-host, port\-only and nickname\-only overrides retain settings. Another \-\-config path selects another saved workspace.
.PP
Session management operations (\-\-resume, \-\-sessions, \-\-detach\-session, \-\-close\-session) are separate: do not combine them or mix them with \-\-config/connection/startup options. Supported \-i may remain in an alias; \-N remains rejected. Management does not load config. \-\-background cannot combine with management or \-\-init\-config/\-\-check\-config. \-\-test\-mode with \-\-init\-config/\-\-check\-config is a usage error.
.PP
Options that take an argument accept \-\-name=value; flags without arguments do not. Ordinary short\-option grouping works. \-\- ends options and positional operands are not used. CLI overrides config, which overrides defaults. Configuration edits require explicit Save; existing saved command\-count files update automatically as described in HISTORY.
.SH "FILES AND ENVIRONMENT"
.PP
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/onekeyirc/config.ini is the default when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is nonempty and absolute. Otherwise $HOME/.config/onekeyirc/config.ini is used; account database information supplies an absolute home if HOME cannot. A nonempty relative XDG_CONFIG_HOME is rejected.
.PP
If the new default is absent, an existing 1keyirc/config.ini under the same configuration base is used in place. No files or credential paths are moved. The new default wins if both exist; an unsafe/unreadable default is reported rather than bypassed. \-\-config FILE overrides default selection.
.PP
For primary path P, P.servers catalogs secondary profile IDs and an optional [servers] default=PROFILE_ID choice; P.d/ contains their private files. /saveconf manages the remembered default. A missing/unreadable default is reported instead of silently choosing another server. P.commands contains canonical command counts; each saved profile's CONFIG_PATH.log is an optional plaintext transcript. See CONFIGURATION and LOGGING for permissions, limits and retention.
.PP
PATH locates optional gpg; the selected executable and the local account are trusted. GNUPGHOME is interpreted by GPG for its local keys/agent. Configured paths do not expand those variables. All paths and networking belong to the host running the client, including over SSH.
.PP
LANG/LC_CTYPE/LC_ALL choose the locale. A valid UTF\-8 locale enables Unicode editing; a non\-UTF\-8/invalid locale selects explicit ASCII terminal mode. Unsupported incoming code points display as '?' without changing stored network text. The client does not guess/transcode legacy encodings. Check locale and locale charmap on the execution host and use an installed locale matching the terminal.
.PP
Command\-form /dcc paths are relative to launch cwd unless absolute. Accept without DIRECTORY uses that directory and requires it to be owned/private. Browsing or backgrounding never changes launch cwd. No incoming file is created before explicit acceptance.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
0 means normal completion/interactive exit. 1 means an operational failure such as setup or verified session control. 2 means invalid usage/configuration or no terminal for interactive use. SIGINT during welcome exits 130 and SIGQUIT during welcome exits 131 after terminal cleanup.
.PP
\-\-background success confirms a local worker, not remote registration. A nonzero close result means cleanup was not confirmed; an accepted request may still complete later, so recheck \-\-sessions.
