.SH "COMMAND REFERENCE"
.SS "/autoauth \- Inspect or prepare SASL authentication"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/autoauth [status|off|sasl USER file PATH|sasl USER gpg PATH]
.PP
Changes the selected server's authentication for its next connection. Verified TLS is required.
.PP
With no argument or status, show connection/authentication state and configured credential file paths, never password contents.
.PP
Current and pending settings are distinguished. Long paths are complete labeled continuation lines.
.PP
PATH is the remaining literal text, including spaces; no shell expansion or quoting is performed.
.PP
No file is opened by this command. GPG keys must be unlocked outside the program.
.PP
Plaintext passwords can be configured explicitly in a private config file, never as command arguments.
.PP
Use /panel for a bouncer profile; /autoauth changes are refused there because its login identity and credential source are managed together.
.PP
Use /save to persist. Disconnect first, then /connect to use applied changes; an active connection is never retargeted.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/autoauth status
/autoauth sasl myaccount gpg /home/me/irc\-account.gpg
/autoauth off
/save
.fi
.RE
.SS "/autocommands \- Prepare delayed commands for the next connection"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/autocommands [list|add COMMAND|remove NUMBER|clear|on|off|delay SECONDS|interval SECONDS]
.PP
Commands belong to the selected server. They run once after registration and required SASL succeeds.
.PP
The default delay is 15 seconds, with at least 2 seconds between completed commands.
.PP
Use delay 0..300 and interval 2..60 to change those timings. A stalled connection never triggers a catch\-up burst.
.PP
Supported commands: /msg, /join, /nick, /away, /back and /whois, including their documented exact aliases.
.PP
Up to 16 commands of 511 UTF\-8 bytes each. One command must fit one IRC message; it is never silently split.
.PP
There is no shell, raw IRC or current\-conversation expansion. Automatic joins do not change the selected view.
.PP
Changes apply to the next connection. /cancel stops still\-unsent startup commands on this connection.
.PP
Lists redact message text and join keys; automatic commands are excluded from history and local message echoes.
.PP
Save with /save. The file must be private (0600 or 0400); an enabled nonempty list requires verified TLS.
.PP
Test mode never runs automatic commands. Startup command text is stored literally in the config, not encrypted by GPG.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/autocommands add /msg ChanServ op #chat mynick
/autocommands delay 15
/autocommands interval 2
/autocommands list
/save
.fi
.RE
.SS "/autoconnect \- Inspect or change automatic startup connection"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/autoconnect [on|off]
.PP
Without an argument, show the selected server's setting. Changes affect the next program startup.
.PP
Use /connect for an immediate connection; use /save to persist the startup choice.
.PP
Each saved server has its own toggle, auto\-join channels and authentication settings.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/autoconnect
/autoconnect on
/save
.fi
.RE
.SS "/autojoin \- Inspect or prepare startup channel joins"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/autojoin [list|add #CHANNEL|remove #CHANNEL|clear]
.PP
With no argument or list, show the selected server, registration/authentication state, running list and pending list.
.PP
Edit the selected server's auto\-join list for its next explicit /connect. Add/remove accepts one channel, without a key.
.PP
Changes are validated before application and do not join or part a live channel.
.PP
Automatic retries keep the running list. Disconnect first, then /connect to apply a pending list.
.PP
Use /join or /part for the running connection, and /save to persist startup changes.
.PP
Bouncer profiles restore their own memberships; /autojoin changes are refused there. Use /join and /part instead.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/autojoin list
/autojoin add #ircnow
/autojoin remove #old
/save
.fi
.RE
.SS "/away \- Set away status, or clear it without a message"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/away [MESSAGE]
.PP
Requires registration. The server confirms the resulting away state. MESSAGE is literal text.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/away Back after lunch
/away
.fi
.RE
.SS "/back \- Clear away status"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/back
.PP
Equivalent to /away with no message. Requires a registered connection.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/back
.fi
.RE
.SS "/background \- Keep connections running and release the terminal"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/background
.PP
Detaches the running session after restoring the terminal. The shell receives this application's numbered resume command.
.PP
From the shell, \-\-sessions lists this program's attached and detached private jobs; \-\-resume resumes its sole available detached job.
.PP
Use \-\-resume NUMBER when more than one job exists. NUMBER is the job ID, not a process ID; it stays stable until exit and may then be reused.
.PP
\-\-detach\-session NUMBER releases an attached terminal/controller but keeps the worker, IRC connections, drafts and history alive for \-\-resume NUMBER.
.PP
\-\-close\-session NUMBER ends the whole job, all IRC connections and transfers, and its unsaved drafts and in\-memory history. Success is reported only after verified completion.
.PP
If resume is busy after a lost SSH terminal, list jobs, detach that job, then resume it. See /help sessions for the recovery sequence.
.PP
An unresponsive worker or a legacy worker lacking a requested control can fail. Installing a binary does not update a running worker; restart it deliberately after an upgrade.
.PP
Resume redraws retained in\-memory scrollback even when no chat arrived while detached. /set single_session on refuses a second background job so bare \-\-resume remains unambiguous.
.PP
To start without a terminal, use \-\-background from the shell; see \-\-help for this program's options.
.PP
When background_away is on and this profile permits automatic away, /background or managed terminal loss queues the configured AWAY after detach. Resume clears only that client\-generated away state; a manual /away is preserved.
.PP
Add \-\-reconnect to retry transient connection failures. See /help deployment and /help reconnect.
.PP
No terminal multiplexer is required. A pending resolver or credential helper can require a retry.
.PP
Suspending an ordinary foreground process pauses its networking. A resumed job can suspend its foreground controller while its managed worker continues networking.
.PP
From the shell: onekeyirc \-\-sessions; onekeyirc \-\-detach\-session NUMBER; onekeyirc \-\-resume NUMBER; onekeyirc \-\-close\-session NUMBER.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/background
.fi
.RE
.SS "/bind \- Assign a custom key to a local program function"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/bind KEY ACTION
.PP
No custom bindings are installed by default. Use /bindings keys and /bindings actions for the supported names.
.PP
The new assignment replaces that key's previous custom action for every server. Other bindings are preserved.
.PP
Bindings run in the main chat and command prompt, not in paste, password/setup fields, readers or confirmation screens.
.PP
Changes take effect now but remain unsaved until /save input. Existing navigation works when a key has no active custom binding.
.PP
Only named local functions are accepted. There is no arbitrary command, macro, send action or shell expansion.
.PP
Terminal\-reserved keys may never reach the program; binding a key does not change terminal signal assignments.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/bindings actions
/bind F7 summary
/bind Alt\-c command
/save input
.fi
.RE
.SS "/bindings \- List custom bindings or available actions and keys"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/bindings [actions|keys]
.PP
Without arguments, list your current application\-wide bindings. An empty list means none are assigned.
.PP
Use /bind to assign or replace a key, /unbind KEY to remove it, and /unbind all to clear the list.
.PP
F1..F12, Ctrl\-A..Ctrl\-Z, Alt\-a..Alt\-z, Alt\-0..Alt\-9 and named navigation keys are supported.
.PP
Ctrl\-H is Backspace, Ctrl\-I is Tab, Ctrl\-J and Ctrl\-M are Enter, and Ctrl\-[ is Escape; these are the same binding.
.PP
Alt letters use unshifted lowercase terminal bytes. Function/Alt key delivery depends on the terminal and any multiplexer.
.PP
Up to 64 bindings are saved as KEY=action in [hotkeys] in the primary config. /save input persists command edits.
.PP
See /help hotkeys for scope, draft protection and terminal signal behavior.
.PP
This client edits bindings through commands; /bindings lists the current table.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/bindings
/bindings keys
/bindings actions
/help hotkeys
.fi
.RE
.SS "/cancel \- Cancel queued chat and startup commands"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/cancel
.PP
Removes chat messages waiting for pacing and stops pending automatic commands on the selected server.
.PP
A frame already being written is allowed to finish.
.PP
Cancellation does not retract messages that the server has already received.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/cancel
.fi
.RE
.SS "/catchup \- Read this conversation from your most recent sent message"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/catchup
.PP
Starts at your most recent locally sent chat/action in the selected channel or query. Server/mentions views have no sent\-chat anchor.
.PP
An eligible private log is tried first. Logging must already have been enabled when the anchor was sent and remain healthy; sensitive authentication text is not a disk anchor. Otherwise retained in\-memory history is used when its anchor is still available.
.PP
The disk reader is a stable snapshot: Up/Down move one row, Page Up/Page Down a page, Home returns to the anchor, End shows the newest snapshot page, and Escape returns live. New traffic does not move that snapshot.
.PP
The in\-memory fallback opens ordinary retained scrollback at the anchor: Home reaches the oldest retained page, and End or Escape returns live. Older retained messages can still be browsed.
.PP
A replaced or changed anchor record cannot be used as the original disk anchor. A fresh process does not restore anchors from old logs. See /help logging and /help history.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/catchup
.fi
.RE
.SS "/channel \- Select a channel; number 0 selects the server"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/channel NUMBER|#CHANNEL
.PP
Numbers are listed by /channels. Selection changes the viewed conversation and subsequent plain\-text target.
.PP
It does not join a channel. A retained parted channel must be rejoined before sending.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/channels
/channel 0
/channel #ircnow
.fi
.RE
.SS "/channels \- List channels on the selected server"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/channels
.PP
Channel 0 is always the server message/MOTD view. Queries have their own /ql list.
.PP
Rows show joined/parted state and unread/mention counts.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/channels
/channel 1
.fi
.RE
.SS "/cl \- List channels (alias of /channels)"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/cl
.PP
Lists this server's channel views, including channel 0 for server messages and each channel's joined/parted state.
.PP
Use /channel NUMBER or /channel #NAME to select one. Queries have their own /ql list.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/cl
/channel 1
.fi
.RE
.SS "/clear \- Clear retained history for the selected view"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/clear
.PP
Clears this view's bounded in\-memory history. It does not part, disconnect, retract sent text or delete an existing persistent log.
.PP
Terminal scrollback already produced by the program is controlled by the terminal.
.PP
In the main chat view, Up opens one\-row retained history, Page Up opens a page, and Home jumps to the oldest retained page; Down/Page Down move newer. New matching traffic does not move the history page being read. End or Escape returns to the live tail; navigation keys do not open the composer.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/clear
.fi
.RE
.SS "/close \- Close a channel or query view"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/close [NAME|NUMBER]
.PP
Without an argument, close the current conversation. A channel is parted before its view is removed.
.PP
The permanent server and mentions views cannot be closed. Channel numbers come from /channels.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/close alice
/close #old
.fi
.RE
.SS "/connect \- Connect directly to a server, or reuse the saved connection"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/connect [\-tls|\-plain] [\-nick NICK] HOST [PORT] | /connect
.PP
Start with /connect irc.example.org. No profile, alias or server number needs to be created first.
.PP
A new server uses verified TLS on port 6697 and your system login nickname, or user when that login is unsuitable.
.PP
An existing matching server keeps its configured port, transport, nickname and authentication.
.PP
Use \-nick NICK for an explicit nickname, or /nick NICK before connecting. Passwords are not positional arguments.
.PP
Use \-plain for explicitly unencrypted IRC (new\-server default port 6667). \-tls and \-ssl explicitly select verified TLS.
.PP
A +PORT also selects TLS. An ordinary numeric port never disables TLS. \-\-tls, \-\-plain and \-\-nick are accepted too.
.PP
The connection becomes selected and opens its server/MOTD view. An already active matching connection is selected without duplication.
.PP
A different endpoint gets fresh authentication settings; another server's credentials and channels are not copied.
.PP
Bare /connect uses the selected server, including applied settings for the next connection. /saveconf remembers the selected default for next launch.
.PP
For bouncer profiles sharing one host, select the intended profile with /server NUMBER, then use bare /connect.
.PP
Existing server numbers are also accepted for compatibility. Test mode remains offline; configuration edits require an explicit save.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/connect irc.example.org
/join #chat
/saveconf
/connect
/connect \-nick mynick irc.example.org +6697
/connect \-plain localhost 6667
.fi
.RE
.SS "/dcc \- Offer, review and manage direct file transfers"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/dcc [list|NICK PATH|send [\-address IPV4] [\-port PORT] NICK PATH|accept NUMBER [DIRECTORY]|reject NUMBER|cancel NUMBER]
.PP
With no arguments, list transfers. /dcc NICK PATH offers a file on the selected server.
.PP
PATH is the remaining literal text, including spaces; it is relative to this process's launch directory.
.PP
The client never changes that directory, including while browsing files or running in the background.
.PP
The short send form advertises the local IPv4 address of the IRC connection. Behind NAT, use \-address with your reachable IPv4 and \-port with a forwarded port.
.PP
No external address\-discovery request is made. IPv6\-only IRC connections need an explicit IPv4 override.
.PP
DCC is an unencrypted direct connection. The peer receives your advertised IP; accepting an offer reveals your IP to that peer.
.PP
Incoming offers stay unopened until /dcc accept NUMBER. DIRECTORY defaults to the launch directory and must be owned by you and private.
.PP
No existing destination is overwritten; symlinks and special files are refused. Offline test mode does no transfer file/socket work.
.PP
Use /dcc reject NUMBER for an incoming offer, or /dcc cancel NUMBER for an active transfer.
.PP
Transfer numbers are shared across this process's servers. Closing an originating server cancels its transfers; disconnecting IRC does not cancel an accepted transfer.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/dcc alice file.txt
/dcc list
/dcc accept 1 ./downloads
/dcc send \-address 203.0.113.20 \-port 5000 alice file.txt
/dcc cancel 2
.fi
.RE
.SS "/disconnect \- Disconnect a server while retaining its profile"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/disconnect [SERVER_NUMBER]
.PP
Without a number, disconnect the selected server. History and configuration remain available.
.PP
This does not change its next\-startup autoconnect setting.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/disconnect
/disconnect 1
.fi
.RE
.SS "/help \- Read topic help, syntax and practical examples"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/help [TOPIC|COMMAND]
.PP
Use /help ls for the topic index, /help commands for command entry and its inventory, or /help autoconnect for one command.
.PP
Request one topic or command name, without arguments. /help /ls explains the local list command; /help ls lists help topics. A leading slash is otherwise optional.
.PP
/help all opens the complete manual in the separate help reader.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/help
/help ls
/help commands
/help autoconnect
/help /ls
.fi
.RE
.SS "/ignore \- Manage local ignored senders"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/ignore [list|clear|[add|remove] MASK]
.PP
Accepts a nickname or nick!user@host mask with * and ? wildcards. Matching user messages/notices are hidden locally.
.PP
Ignored text does not create queries or mentions. Protocol state, server notices and membership still work.
.PP
Use /save to persist this server's masks.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/ignore list
/ignore add noisy!*@*
/ignore remove noisy!*@*
.fi
.RE
.SS "/join \- Join one channel on the selected server"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/join #CHANNEL [KEY]
.PP
Requires registration. A key is one printable ASCII token and is never saved or retained in command history.
.PP
On a direct connection, the channel name becomes part of the running auto\-join list; separately staged /autojoin edits take precedence when saved.
.PP
Use /autojoin to edit a direct profile's next explicit connection without joining now. A bouncer manages its own memberships.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/join #ircnow
/join #private channel\-key
.fi
.RE
.SS "/list \- Request and filter the network channel directory"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/list [MASK] [\-\-min\-users N] [\-\-max\-users N] [\-\-name TEXT] [\-\-topic TEXT] [\-\-sort channel|users|topic] [\-\-asc|\-\-desc] [\-\-limit N] | /list show [\-\-sort channel|users|topic] [\-\-asc|\-\-desc] [\-\-limit N] | /list clear
.PP
Requests channels from the selected IRC server; registration is required. MASK is the only filter sent to the server.
.PP
Minimum/maximum users, name and topic filters are applied locally so they work even when a network lacks extended LIST filtering.
.PP
\-\-sort selects channel, users or topic; users descending is the default. \-\-limit 0 means no display limit within the bounded cache.
.PP
/list show prints the cached directory; its \-\-sort, \-\-asc/\-\-desc and \-\-limit options reorder or trim the cache locally without another IRC request. /list clear removes the cache.
.PP
Disconnect or a server LIST rejection keeps any partial cache but releases the request; retry /list after registration, or use /list clear.
.PP
The full\-screen client opens the sortable channel browser; minimalist mode uses the same options and /list show.
.PP
Use /ls or /cl for local channel views; /list searches the network.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/list
/list *linux* \-\-min\-users 5 \-\-max\-users 500
/list \-\-topic help \-\-sort users \-\-desc \-\-limit 100
/list show \-\-sort channel \-\-asc \-\-limit 50
/list clear
.fi
.RE
.SS "/ls \- List server, channel, query or unread entries"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/ls [servers|channels|queries|unread]
.PP
Without an argument, list channels on the selected server. This is a local client list command, not a shell command or a network\-wide IRC LIST request.
.PP
Use /server N, /channel N and /pm NICK to select the corresponding entries.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/ls
/ls servers
/ls queries
/ls unread
.fi
.RE
.SS "/manual \- Read the complete built\-in manual"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/manual
.PP
The same topic and command help is available without connecting. /help TOPIC is the shorter route.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/manual
.fi
.RE
.SS "/me \- Send an action to the selected conversation"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/me TEXT
.PP
Requires a joined channel or query and a registered connection. Message\-budget and send\-mode rules apply.
.PP
The frontend reviews a split message before queueing it.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/me waves
.fi
.RE
.SS "/mentions \- View mentions or clear retained mentions across servers"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/mentions [clear]
.PP
Selects this server's mentions view. It contains bounded recent source lines and their original timestamps.
.PP
/mentions clear removes retained mention copies across all current servers without switching views.
.PP
Source conversation history, unread counts and pending work are preserved.
.PP
Use /watch for additional literal strings; /channel or /pm returns to a sendable conversation.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/mentions
/mentions clear
/channel #ircnow
.fi
.RE
.SS "/msg \- Send text to one nickname or channel"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/msg TARGET TEXT
.PP
Requires registration. This command creates a send plan; direct/review mode and split\-message consent still apply.
.PP
It does not silently change the current send target. Service/credential messages are excluded from command history.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/msg alice Hello
/msg #ircnow Good evening
.fi
.RE
.SS "/next \- Select the next conversation on this server"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/next
.PP
Cycles server, mentions, channel and query views; the internal manual buffer is omitted.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/next
.fi
.RE
.SS "/nick \- Set or request the current nickname"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/nick NICK
.PP
While connected, requests a server\-confirmed change. While disconnected, updates the next connection identity.
.PP
Nicknames use the supported IRC ASCII syntax and are limited to 63 bytes.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/nick tutankh\-aten
.fi
.RE
.SS "/panel \- Open the interactive configuration panel"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/panel
.PP
Choose 0 (the default) for application preferences, or profile panels 1..8; panel 1 edits the primary. These are panel choices, not shell job IDs.
.PP
Application preferences contain history retention, send mode, output format, queue notices and command suggestions for all servers.
.PP
They work without a configured server. Their final choices are 1 Save (default) and 2 Apply without saving; neither connects.
.PP
A server panel contains only its connection, startup, authentication, mention and ignore settings.
.PP
Choosing any panel does not switch your chat conversation.
.PP
Enter keeps a shown value; '\-' clears an optional field. Escape returns one question at a time; repeat it to leave without applying edits.
.PP
Tab selects the question heading. Press ? there for help about the option and its screen; Tab or Enter resumes the answer.
.PP
Every editable answer keeps ? as literal text, including the first character and wildcard masks.
.PP
Watch terms and ignore masks are entered one at a time; an empty new entry finishes that list.
.PP
Credential input is hidden and setup answers never enter command history. Existing credential files are not read while editing.
.PP
For a GPG source, choose Create new or Use existing. Creation offers a private default path; Enter accepts it and prepares its directory (0700).
.PP
A different destination must be in an existing directory you own with mode 0700. The path is checked before the key and password questions.
.PP
Creation then asks for a full local public\-key fingerprint and the password twice. Existing files and symlinks are never replaced.
.PP
Credential paths are stored as absolute paths. /autoauth status shows their complete configured locations without reading their contents.
.PP
Encryption starts only after Finish, keeps IRC responsive, and never uses a plaintext temporary file. Escape cancels pending work.
.PP
A failed config save keeps completed ciphertext available for a deliberate retry; files referenced by a saved config are preserved.
.PP
Test mode does not start GPG or create credential files. Unlock the matching private key externally before authenticating.
.PP
A server panel's final choices are 1 Save and connect, 2 Save only (default), 3 Connect once, or 4 Apply only.
.PP
Nothing is applied, saved or connected merely by opening the panel or answering its earlier questions.
.PP
Connect once applies without saving; Apply only neither saves nor connects. A live connection is never implicitly retargeted or reconnected.
.PP
A failed connection does not undo changes already applied or saved by the final action.
.PP
Use /quickstart for the shorter server, transport, port and nickname setup.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/panel
.fi
.RE
.SS "/part \- Leave a channel with an optional reason and retain its history"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/part [#CHANNEL] [MESSAGE]
.PP
Without a channel, parts the selected joined channel. Text after a channel is the PART reason; text without a channel is the reason for the selected channel.
.PP
If no explicit reason is supplied, part_message is used when configured. The running auto\-join entry is removed.
.PP
Queued messages for that channel are cancelled; one in\-flight frame may finish. Use /close to remove the view.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/part
/part Going offline
/part #old Moving elsewhere
.fi
.RE
.SS "/pm \- Select or open a private query without sending"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/pm NICK|NUMBER
.PP
Names open or select a query. Numbers select an existing query from this server's /ql list.
.PP
Creating a query does not contact the nickname until a message is sent. Numbers can change after closing a query.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/pm alice
/ql
/pm 1
.fi
.RE
.SS "/prev \- Select the previous conversation on this server"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/prev
.PP
The reverse of /next. It does not connect, join or send.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/prev
.fi
.RE
.SS "/ql \- List private queries on the selected server"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/ql
.PP
Query numbers start at 1 and are separate from channel numbering. Use /query NICK or /query NUMBER to select one.
.PP
Use /close NICK to close a query; run /ql again after closing because subsequent query numbers may change.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/ql
/query 1
/query alice
.fi
.RE
.SS "/queries \- List private queries (alias of /ql)"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/queries
.PP
Uses the same private query list as /ql. Channel numbering remains separate.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/queries
/pm alice
.fi
.RE
.SS "/query \- Open or select a private conversation"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/query NICK|NUMBER
.PP
Selects a private conversation without sending a message. Type normally to compose to that nickname.
.PP
Use /ql to list numbered queries and /close NICK to close one. /pm accepts the same names and numbers.
.PP
A number must refer to an existing query on the selected server; 0 never selects or creates a query.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/query alice
/ql
/query 1
/close alice
.fi
.RE
.SS "/quickstart \- Open guided connection setup"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/quickstart
.PP
Edit the current profile's server, TLS transport, port and nickname in that order, then review the settings.
.PP
Verified TLS is the default. A plaintext connection must be selected explicitly.
.PP
Enter keeps a shown value. Escape returns one question at a time; repeat it to leave without applying edits. Answers never enter command history.
.PP
At the final review choose 1 Save and connect, 2 Save only, 3 Connect once (default), or 4 Apply only.
.PP
Opening setup and answering earlier questions does not apply settings, save a file or connect.
.PP
Connect once applies without saving; Apply only neither saves nor connects. A live connection is never implicitly retargeted or reconnected.
.PP
A failed connection does not undo changes already applied or saved by the final action.
.PP
Use /panel for bouncer profiles and broader configuration choices. Quickstart does not edit an existing bouncer login.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/quickstart
.fi
.RE
.SS "/quit \- Exit the program and close all sessions"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/quit [MESSAGE]
.PP
Sends MESSAGE as the best\-effort IRC QUIT reason on each live server, or quit_message when MESSAGE is omitted, then restores the terminal and exits.
.PP
Use /background to leave connections running instead.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/quit
/quit Client restart
.fi
.RE
.SS "/save \- Save the current server, application preferences, or all profiles"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/save [input|output|all]
.PP
Bare /save writes the selected server's effective settings, including staged connection changes.
.PP
/save input and /save output both write the primary effective profile, including application preferences and staged primary connection settings. They do not save only the named subset.
.PP
/save all saves each healthy profile separately. If one fails, earlier successful files remain saved and failures are reported.
.PP
Each file replacement is private and atomic; this is not one transaction across all files. Test mode never writes configs.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/save
/set send_mode review
/save input
/save all
.fi
.RE
.SS "/saveconf \- Save configuration and remember the selected connection"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/saveconf
.PP
Saves application preferences and all healthy server configurations, then remembers the selected server as the default.
.PP
Next time you start the program, /connect uses that saved default; no server number or separate setup command is needed.
.PP
Nickname, joined channels, authentication and startup choices are included; explicit staged connection edits take precedence.
.PP
This does not turn on automatic startup connection. Use /autoconnect on first if you want that behavior.
.PP
Each file is replaced privately and atomically. Successful writes remain saved if another file fails.
.PP
The selected default is remembered if its own save succeeds; failures are reported and unreadable profiles are not overwritten.
.PP
Test mode never writes configuration. /save retains its more targeted forms for individual profiles and preferences.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/connect irc.example.org
/join #chat
/saveconf
/quit
.fi
.RE
.SS "/server \- Connect to a server, or select an existing one"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/server [\-tls|\-plain] [\-nick NICK] HOST [PORT] | /server [NUMBER|add HOST [PORT] [NICK]|connect NUMBER|disconnect NUMBER|close NUMBER]
.PP
With a hostname, this is the same direct connection command as /connect. No prior profile creation is needed.
.PP
No argument selects the current server's message/MOTD view. NUMBER selects a server slot without connecting it.
.PP
add creates a profile with fresh credentials, verified TLS and autoconnect off; it neither selects, connects nor saves it.
.PP
PORT defaults to 6697. A different port does not disable TLS; plaintext must be explicitly configured.
.PP
connect/disconnect do not change selection. close removes a secondary session/catalog entry, not its profile file.
.PP
Minimalist server numbers are 1..8, with primary 1. The primary remains available; disconnect it instead of closing it.
.PP
Numbers remain stable for the lifetime of their sessions. Panel 0 is reserved for application preferences.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/server irc.example.org
/join #chat
/saveconf
/servers
/server 1
.fi
.RE
.SS "/servers \- List all configured server sessions"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/servers
.PP
Shows minimalist server slots 1..8 (primary 1), selected server, connection state and per\-server autoconnect choice.
.PP
A loaded profile stays available even when it is not connected.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/servers
/server 1
.fi
.RE
.SS "/set \- Choose application\-wide input, output and presence preferences"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/set send_mode direct|review | /set composer_mode line|fullscreen | /set output_format compact|full | /set queue_notices on|off | /set program_messages on|off | /set persistent_logging on|off | /set single_session on|off | /set default_interface idle|ncurses | /set command_suggestions alphabetical|common|history|frequent|contextual | /set part_message [TEXT] | /set quit_message [TEXT] | /set background_away on|off | /set background_away_message TEXT | /set autoaway_minutes 0..1440 | /set autoaway_message TEXT
.PP
direct is the default: Enter submits the composition, with mandatory consent if it needs multiple IRC messages.
.PP
review adds a deliberate review step before ordinary messages are queued.
.PP
output_format compact is the default: selected chat shows [HH:MM:SS] <nick> text.
.PP
output_format full includes the server, date and target on each chat line. Mentions and other\-server diagnostics retain necessary origin context in compact mode.
.PP
queue_notices off hides routine queued/paced acknowledgements. In the minimalist frontend, an explicitly reviewed send omits its redundant routine queue acknowledgement even when this setting is on. Errors and multi\-message confirmation always remain visible.
.PP
program_messages off hides low\-priority local feedback such as draft\-cancelled, input\-cleared and preserved\-draft notices. Errors and safety confirmations remain visible.
.PP
persistent_logging on appends retained server/conversation lines to an owned 0600 .log file in the saved configuration's owned 0700 directory. It is off by default. Each log stops at 16 MiB; archive it and toggle logging off/on to resume. Unsafe files or a busy log stop logging with an error; live chat continues.
.PP
single_session on permits only one background job in this client namespace, so a bare \-\-resume is unambiguous. Multiple IRC servers remain available inside that session.
.PP
default_interface controls the next fresh launch where interface selection is supported. It does not switch the current interface. /save input persists this application preference.
.PP
command_suggestions chooses alphabetical order, common commands, recent command history, most\-used commands, or commands relevant to the originating screen.
.PP
composer_mode line keeps one input row below the live tail; fullscreen keeps a multi\-line editor while reserving a live\-tail pane. Line is the default.
.PP
part_message and quit_message provide default IRC departure reasons; an explicit /part or /quit message overrides them. Empty part_message or quit_message disables that default text.
.PP
background_away sends background_away_message after a successful /background or managed terminal loss. autoaway_minutes sets local\-idle auto\-away; 0 disables it.
.PP
Only client\-generated away state is cleared automatically on resume or genuine local input. A manual /away is never overwritten or cleared by these features.
.PP
Common is the default. Contextual prioritizes channel, query, server or client commands while retaining matching commands from other groups.
.PP
Common uses a curated useful order. Contextual remembers the actual screen that opened command entry, including a client\-wide summary reader.
.PP
These are application preferences and apply across servers. Use /save input or /save output to persist them.
.PP
/panel, followed by 0, edits these preferences and the global history limit together.
.PP
onekeyirc always uses idle. Its application panel omits interface selection; /set default_interface accepts only idle.
.PP
The shared config parser preserves either recognized saved value. A stored ncurses value does not change this standalone client's interface.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/set send_mode review
/set composer_mode line
/set command_suggestions contextual
/save input
/set output_format full
/set queue_notices off
/set program_messages off
/set persistent_logging on
/set single_session on
/save output
.fi
.RE
.SS "/settings \- Show effective settings with credentials redacted"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/settings
.PP
Shows application preferences separately from the selected server's pending connection choices.
.PP
Authentication values are reported by source type; passwords and credential paths are never printed.
.PP
Use /autoconnect, /autojoin, /autoauth and /set to change their respective settings.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/settings
/help configuration
.fi
.RE
.SS "/sl \- List servers (alias of /servers)"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/sl
.PP
Uses the same configured\-server list and stable slot numbers as /servers.
.PP
Use /server NUMBER to select one.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/sl
/server 1
.fi
.RE
.SS "/soju \- Control the selected soju bouncer"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/soju [COMMAND]
.PP
Requires a connected soju profile configured in /panel. No argument asks BouncerServ for help.
.PP
COMMAND is sent as one paced private message, without local echo or command history.
.PP
A direct IRC connection cannot use this shortcut. Replies appear in the server view.
.PP
Use the bouncer's own help for account and network commands.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/soju
/soju network status
.fi
.RE
.SS "/summary \- Summarize all current server sessions and activity"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/summary
.PP
Shows every server, channel 0, channel/query views, unread activity, mentions, outgoing queues, startup commands and file transfers.
.PP
This is a local snapshot and works while disconnected. Press r in its reader, or run it again, to refresh.
.PP
Opening it does not change your conversation or mark anything read. The copied mentions feed is excluded from activity totals.
.PP
Mentions may also be unread messages; these totals are not added together. Joined and left channel views are identified separately.
.PP
The report uses its own scrollable reader and preserves drafts. Slash opens command entry; cancelling returns to the snapshot.
.PP
A failed refresh retains the previous snapshot. No network request is made by opening or refreshing it.
.PP
Credentials and the contents of queued messages or automatic commands are never printed.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/summary
.fi
.RE
.SS "/topic \- Request or change the current channel topic"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/topic [TEXT]
.PP
Requires a joined channel and registration. Without text, requests the topic; otherwise submits literal TEXT.
.PP
The server decides whether your current channel privileges permit a change.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/topic
/topic Welcome to our channel
.fi
.RE
.SS "/transfers \- Manage file transfers (alias of /dcc)"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/transfers [ARGUMENTS]
.PP
The same commands and protections as /dcc. Use /help dcc for send, receive and cancellation syntax.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/transfers
/transfers alice file.txt
.fi
.RE
.SS "/unbind \- Remove one or all custom key bindings"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/unbind KEY|all
.PP
Removing a key restores its ordinary behavior wherever it had a custom action. all clears only the custom binding list.
.PP
The change applies across servers immediately. Use /save input to retain it after restart.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/unbind F7
/unbind all
/save input
.fi
.RE
.SS "/unignore \- Remove one local ignore mask"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/unignore MASK
.PP
Equivalent to /ignore remove MASK. Save the selected profile to persist the change.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/unignore noisy!*@*
.fi
.RE
.SS "/unread \- List unread conversations across servers"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/unread
.PP
Rows include the server number, conversation name, unread messages and mention counts.
.PP
Use /server NUMBER followed by /channel or /pm to select a conversation.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/unread
/server 1
/pm alice
.fi
.RE
.SS "/users \- Print the current or named channel's known users"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/users [#CHANNEL]
.PP
This is a textual roster, not a visibility toggle. User mode prefixes are included when known.
.PP
The roster opens in a separate reader, even when empty. Enter, Escape or q returns to the selected chat log.
.PP
Up/Down and Page Up/Down scroll the roster. In Help, Enter continues scrolling.
.PP
An incomplete or capacity\-limited NAMES reply is labelled. It does not send WHOIS requests.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/users
/users #ircnow
.fi
.RE
.SS "/view \- Select a named conversation or numbered channel"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/view NAME|NUMBER
.PP
Channel numbers are listed by /channels; query names by /ql. The server and mentions names are also accepted.
.PP
Use /help instead of selecting an internal manual buffer.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/view #ircnow
/view alice
/view 0
.fi
.RE
.SS "/views \- List channels (alias of /channels)"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/views
.PP
This alias uses channel numbering; private queries remain in /ql.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/views
.fi
.RE
.SS "/watch \- Manage extra literal mention strings"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/watch [list|add TEXT|remove TEXT]
.PP
Nickname mentions always remain enabled. Add/remove treats the remainder as literal text, not a regular expression.
.PP
Up to 32 additional strings of 1 to 127 UTF\-8 bytes are supported per server. Use /save to persist.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/watch list
/watch add release day
/watch remove release day
/mentions
.fi
.RE
.SS "/whois \- Request nickname information from the server"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/whois NICK
.PP
Requires registration. Selects the server view so its response is visible. No local command is executed.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/whois alice
.fi
.RE
.SS "/znc \- Control the selected ZNC bouncer"
.PP
.B Usage:
.PP
/znc [COMMAND]
.PP
Requires a connected ZNC profile configured in /panel. No argument asks *status for Help.
.PP
COMMAND is sent as one paced private message, without local echo or command history.
.PP
Use /msg *module COMMAND for an enabled ZNC module. Module replies appear in the server view.
.PP
A direct IRC connection cannot use this shortcut. Use the bouncer's help for its commands.
.PP
.B Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
/znc
/znc ListNetworks
/znc Connect
.fi
.RE
